const body = "---\ntitle: Plan your topics\ndescription: Identify reader needs, choose topic types, and divide an unstructured passage into focused DITA topics before writing XML.\ntype: tutorial\n---\n\n# Plan your topics {#plan-your-topics}\n\nCreate a small content plan before writing XML. Each topic should answer a reader's question or help the reader complete a task. A map later assembles these topics for an audience.\n\n**Time:** about 15 minutes. **You need:** a text editor. No DITA tools are required for this exercise.\n\n## Identify the reader and result {#identify-the-reader-and-result}\n\nFor this guide, assume the reader is new to audio editing and wants to record, trim, and export a first track. Write that outcome at the top of your plan. Exclude advanced production advice from the initial workflow.\n\n## Divide the source material {#divide-the-source-material}\n\nUse this unstructured passage as your source:\n\n> An audio project stores tracks and editing decisions. Connect a microphone before starting. Select the recording device, record a short sample, and play it back. WAV and FLAC are suitable for a lossless export; MP3 is a distribution option. If playback is silent, check the output device and track mute settings. Save the project so that you can continue editing.\n\nCreate a content plan with a working title, reader question, topic type, and scope for each topic. Use this decision table:\n\n| Reader need | Topic type | Example title |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Understand a subject or make sense of a task | Concept | What an audio project contains |\n| Complete an action with a result | Task | Recording a test track |\n| Look up facts or compare values | Reference | Export formats |\n| Diagnose a symptom and try remedies | Troubleshooting | Playback is silent |\n\nSeparate prerequisites from actions. A connected microphone is a prerequisite; selecting a recording device is an action. Place facts that support several tasks in a concept or reference and link to them.\n\n## Choose topic boundaries {#choose-topic-boundaries}\n\nUse a separate task when the reader has a distinct goal, prerequisite, or result. Keep a short explanation in the task's context when readers need it only for that procedure. A section can organize a topic; it does not by itself justify creating another file.\n\nReview the plan against these questions:\n\n- Can the reader predict the content from the title?\n- Can each task be completed without following unrelated instructions?\n- Does each topic make sense when linked from a different guide?\n- Have you repeated information that one shared topic could supply?\n\n## Check the result {#check-the-result}\n\nA reasonable plan has a concept about projects, a recording task, an export reference, and a troubleshooting topic. Saving can remain a recording step for this short example. A larger save procedure could become its own task. Different boundaries are acceptable when you can explain the reader need.\n\nContinue with [Stage 00](/dita-tutorial/part-1-topics/stage-00-setup), then write the first concept in [Stage 01](/dita-tutorial/part-1-topics/stage-01-concept).\n";
